# Untitled

## Dimensional reduction enhances photocatalytic carbon dioxide reduction performance of metal-organic frameworks
Kaiyue Ma, Jixin Li, Jinlu Liu, Chunguang Li, Zhan Shi (✉), and Shouhua Feng
State Key Laboratory of Inorganic Synthesis and Preparative Chemistry, College of Chemistry, Jilin University, Changchun 130012, China
\(\copyright\) Tsinghua University Press 2023
## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have shown significant potential as photocatalysts. It has been widely assumed that all catalytic active sites within MOFs are functional in photocatalytic reactions but for a three-dimensional MOF, whether the internal catalytic active sites can effectively absorb light and actively contribute to photocatalytic reactions remains to be explored. In this context, we synthesized a two-dimensional nanosheet MOF (2D-MOF) and a three-dimensional bulk MOF (3D-MOF) composed of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and tetracarboxylic porphyrin (TCPP) by the approach described in the literature. \(\mathsf{R e}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{C l}\) \((\mathsf{b p y}=2,2^{\prime}\) - bipyridine), which has remarkable \(C O_{2}\) photoreduction ability, was introduced to the two MOFs to create two new photocatalysts 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re, respectively. Photocatalytic \(C O_{2}\) reduction experiments show that based on the equal number of catalytic active sites, the CO turnover number (TON) of 2D-MOF-Re reaches 27.8 in \(6\,\mathrm{h}\) , which is 50 times that of 3D-MOF-Re. The result shows that certain catalytic active sites inside the bulk MOF are inactive due to the inability to absorb light. This study illuminates the potential of the dimensional reduction approach in the design of photocatalysts to exploit the capabilities fully.
## KEYWORDS
photocatalysis, \(C O_{2}\) reduction, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), nanosheets, light absorption
## 1 Introduction
With the increasing urgency of global climate change and energy demand, seeking sustainable energy solutions and measures to reduce greenhouse gas emissions is crucial [1–3]. Photocatalytic \(C O_{2}\) reduction, as a potential technological pathway, aims to convert atmospheric \(C O_{2}\) into valuable chemicals for efficient carbon resource utilization [4–7]. However, achieving efficient photocatalytic \(C O_{2}\) reduction still faces many challenges, one of which is the development of catalysts with excellent photocatalytic performance [8]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a highly promising class of catalysts for \(C O_{2}\) photoreduction [9–13]. They are porous materials composed of metal clusters and organic ligands, which are interconnected alternately, providing excellent design flexibility. Researchers can choose metal clusters and/or organic ligands with \(C O_{2}\) reduction abilities to construct MOFs, where these clusters or ligands serve as catalytic active sites, providing MOFs with the capability of \(C O_{2}\) photoreduction [14–18]. The porous structure of MOFs can ensure that each catalytic active site is exposed, and consequently all active sites inside bulk MOFs tend to be assumed to be catalytically active [19, 20].
When MOFs are used as catalysts in thermocatalytic reactions, thermal energy can conveniently be transferred to every catalytic active site of MOFs through molecular vibrations [21–23]. In this case, the catalytic active sites inside MOFs can undoubtedly play a catalytic role. However, in photocatalytic reactions, light serves as the energy source for the reaction. Whether the light energy can penetrate the outer layer of MOFs and reach the catalytic active sites inside MOFs, allowing these sites to absorb and function, is still debatable [24, 25].
