Some previous research has also focused on enhancing the light absorption properties of MOFs [26–29]. To enhance the light absorption properties of MOFs, the size of MOFs can be reduced. A typical example is metal-organic cages (MOCs), which are also porous materials formed by alternately connecting metal clusters and organic ligands and, therefore, can be regarded as a particular form of MOFs. When MOCs are dissolved in certain solvents, they can be dispersed into independent cages, leading to an enhancement of their light absorption properties and consequently, an improvement in catalytic performance [30–32]. Another prominent example is two-dimensional MOF nanosheets, where one dimension of MOFs is reduced to only one or a few layers, which can enhance their light absorption capacity and promote catalytic reactions [33–36]. However, these studies have only provided a simple analysis of the benefits of reducing the size of MOFs [37, 38], and there is still a lack of systematic investigation on the effects of MOF size on light absorption ability of their catalytic active sites and subsequently on their catalytic performance.
Based on the above discussions, we selected two-dimensional and bulk-phase MOFs composed of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and carboxylic porphyrin ligands as the primary research objects. The large-sized porphyrin ligands ensure sufficient pore space for material transport inside bulk-phase MOFs, minimizing the impact of mass transfer processes on catalytic activity. To demonstrate the difference in catalytic performance, we incorporated \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) functional units with excellent \(C O_{2}\) reduction
ability [39–42] into the porphyrin MOFs. The photocatalytic experimental results demonstrate that, with an equal number of catalytic active sites, the dimensionally reduced MOFs exhibit orders of magnitude increase in catalytic activity. The two-dimensional MOF nanosheets (2D-MOF-Re) exhibited superb catalytic activity with a CO production turnover number (TON) reaching 27.8 in \(6\;\mathrm{h}\), which is 50 times that of bulk-phase MOF (3D-MOF-Re), indicating that the light absorption properties of MOFs significantly impact their catalytic activity. Catalytically active sites inside large-sized MOFs may fail to work due to the inability to absorb light, which indeed leads to the wastage of catalytic active sites.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of 2D-MOF
2D-MOF was synthesized as detailed in the previously reported literature [36]. \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) (50 mg, 0.21 mmol), acetic acid (HAc, 600 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\)), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (255 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\)), and N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) (10 mL) were ultrasonically dissolved in a 20 mL glass vial. Tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP) (50 mg, 0.06 mmol) was added after the above reaction mixture became clear. The resulting solution was heated at \(120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). After cooling down to room temperature, dark purple crystals were harvested by centrifugation (8000 rpm, 30 min) and then washed with DMF three times and acetone once.
## 2.2 Synthesis of 2D-MOF-Re
2D-MOF-Re was synthesized by two steps. First of all, 2D-MOF (21.3 mg) and 2,2'-bipyridine-5-carboxylic acid (bpscy) (10 mg) were added to ethanol (10 mL). The mixture was stirred at room temperature overnight. Then Re \((\mathrm{CO})_{5}\mathrm{Cl}\) (10 mg) was added to the above suspension. The mixture was stirred at \(70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for another 12 h. The dark red precipitate was collected by centrifugation (8000 rpm, 30 min) and washed with ethanol three times. Re content of the 2D-MOF-Re was 9.57 %, as determined by inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES).
## 2.3 Synthesis of 3D-MOF (PCN-224)
3D-MOF was synthesized based on the reported literature [37]. \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) (120 mg) and benzoic acid (1.6 g) were ultrasonically dissolved in DMF (4 mL). And TCPP (40 mg) was dispersed in another DMF (4 mL). The two solutions were charged and mixed thoroughly in a 20 mL Teflon-lined autoclave and heated in a 120 °C oven for 24 h. After cooling to room temperature, dark purple crystals were harvested by centrifugation (8000 rpm, 5 min) and then washed with DMF three times and acetone once. Re content of the 3D-MOF-Re was 4.55%, as determined by ICP-OES.
another DMF \(\mathrm{4~mL}\) ). The two solutions were charged and mixed thoroughly in a \(20\,\mathrm{\mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave and heated in a \(120~^{\circ}\!C\) oven for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . After cooling to room temperature, dark purple crystals were harvested by centrifugation \((8000~\mathrm{\rpm},\) \(5\,\mathrm{{min})}\) and then washed with DMF three times and acetone once.
## 2.4 Synthesis of 3D-MOF-Re
\(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) (60 mg) and benzoic acid (1.8 g) were ultrasonically dissolved in DMF (4 mL). TCPP (30 mg) and Re(bpcdy)(CO)Cl (bpcdy = 2,2'-bipyridine-5,5'-carboxylic acid) (30 mg) were dispersed in another DMF (4 mL). The two solutions were charged and mixed thoroughly in a 20 mL Teflon-lined autoclave and heated in a 120 °C oven for 24 h. After cooling to room temperature, dull red crystals were harvested by centrifugation (8000 rpm, 5 min) and washed with DMF three times and acetone once. Re content of the 3D-MOF-Re was 4.55%, as determined by ICP-OES.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Materials characterization
2D-MOF was then step-wisely reacted with bpscy and \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{CO})_{5}\mathrm{Cl}\), leading to the replacement of some acetate groups on
\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) clusters of 2D-MOF by \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) to form 2D-MOF-Re (Scheme 1 and Fig. S2 in the ESM). ICP-OES analysis of digested 2D-MOF-Re showed a Re content of \(9.57\%\) , which gives an empirical formula of 2D-MOF-Re, thus approximated as \(\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(T C P P)(C H_{3}C O)_{2}O_{2.865}(C_{14}H_{7}N_{2}O_{5}C l R e)_{1.135}}\) . The
PXRD pattern indicates that 2D-MOF-Re is isoreticular to 2DMOF (Fig. 1(a)) and the similar size and morphology to 2D-MOF were confirmed by TEM (Fig. 1(f)). Meanwhile, scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) and energy dispersive X-ray (EDS) elemental mapping of 2D-MOF-Re illustrated a uniform distribution of C, N, Cl, O, Zr, and Re, suggesting the introduction of \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) (Fig. S3 in the ESM). Atomic force microscopy (AFM) showed an average thickness of \(6\,\mathrm{nm}\) for 2D-MOF-Re, equivalent to the thickness of 2–3 molecular layers (Fig. S4 in the ESM). It is slightly thicker than the theoretical singlelayer thickness, which may be caused by sample deposition during preparation. The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum of 2D-MOF-Re (Fig. 1(b)) showed new peaks at 1891, 1921, and \(2024\ \ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) corresponding to the CO stretching bands of \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) complex [47–49]. Additionally, compared to the \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectrum of digested 2D-MOF (Fig. S7 in the ESM), the \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR spectrum of digested 2D-MOF-Re showed new significant signals attributed to
\(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) (Fig. 1(c)). These two experimental results further confirm the successful incorporation of \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) in 2D-MOF-Re.
As a control, 3D-MOF-Re was synthesized through a one-pot reaction between \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) and two mixed ligands, \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpdcy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) \((\mathrm{bpdcy}=5,5^{\prime}\) -dicarboxyl- \(\cdot2,2^{\prime}\) -bipyridine) and TCPP with benzoic acid as modulators (Scheme 1). The PXRD patterns (Fig. 1(g)) indicate that 3D-MOF-Re possesses high crystallinity and is isostructural to PCN-224 (referred to as 3D-MOF here) [50]. 3D-MOF is a 3D crystalline MOF consisting of 6-connected \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) clusters and TCPP as SBUs to afford a molecular formula of \(\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(T C P P)_{1.5}(O H)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{6}}\) . 3D-MOF-Re, based on the 3DMOF framework, was further formed by linking two carboxylic groups of \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpdcy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) to uncoordinated sites on the \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) clusters (Fig. S8 in the ESM) to afford an experimental formula of \(\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(T C P P)_{1.5}(O H)_{4.86}(H_{2}O)_{4.86}(C_{15}H_{6}N_{2}O_{7}C l R e)_{0.57}}\) based on the ICP-OES analysis of digested 3D-MOF-Re with a Re content of \(4.55\%\) . The 3D-MOF-Re also showed CO stretching vibrations similar to those of \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpdcy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl},\) indicating the presence of Re moiety within the structure (Fig. 1(h)). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image (Fig. 1(i)) reveals a bulk structure of 3D-MOF-Re with a size of approximately \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) . The elemental distribution of 3D-MOF-Re was analyzed using SEM
EDS mapping; however, this was hampered by the buried nature of Re units, making the elemental distribution of Re indiscernible (Fig. S9 in the ESM). To overcome the limitation, 3D-MOF-Re was ground to expose Re units on the surface, enabling a more precise TEM-EDS mapping analysis and providing a clear view of Re element distribution (Fig. S10 in the ESM). These complementary techniques offered essential insights into the distribution of Re elements within the 3D-MOF-Re framework.
Re coordination environments of 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re were studied by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements. 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re showed similar XPS peak profiles with two obvious Re \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) and Re \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) peaks appearing at the binding energy of 44.27 and \(41.82~\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. 2(a)), suggesting the uniform coordination environments of Re(I) centers [41, 51, 52]. Furthermore, compared to the corresponding monomers, \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscyc})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) and \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscyc})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\), the binding energies of 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re are separately shifted by 0.40 and \(0.22~\mathrm{eV}\) (Figs. S11 and S12 in the ESM), which probably attribute to the coordination of the carboxyl group to \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and eventually affect the coordination field strength [49]. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm indicates that 3D-MOF-Re possesses high porosity with a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of \(1768~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and the maximum pore size is estimated to be \(1.6\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S13 in the ESM), which is substantially larger than the dimensions of triethanolamine (TEOA) and 1,3-dimethyl-2-phenyl-2,3-dihydro-1H-benzo[d]imidazole (BIH) (Fig. S14 in the ESM). 2D-MOF-Re shows a typical type II isotherm corresponding to non-porous materials (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms demonstrate that 3D-MOF-Re has almost the same \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity as 2D-MOF-Re (Figs. 2(b) and 2(c)). Gas adsorption test results indicate that the structure of 3D-MOF-Re can minimize the impact of diffusion and mass transfer processes on its catalytic activity, which is also an advantage of 2D materials in catalysis.
## 3.2 Photocatalytic \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) production measurements
2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re provide an exceptional opportunity to examine whether light absorbance capacity is critical in driving effective photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction. Visible-light driven photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction experiments were conducted under \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) atmosphere in \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) with TEOA and BIH as sacrificial reagents. According to the Re content determined by ICP-OES, two samples with the same molar amount \((10\ \ \upmu\mathrm{mol})\) of Re were used in the photocatalytic experiments to ensure a consistent number of catalytic centers. The gas products were on line quantified by gas chromatography (GC) during the catalytic processes. As shown in Fig. 3(a), 2D-MOF-Re shows a TON of 27.8 and a high CO selectivity of \(97\%\) in three parallel experiments, which outperforms most previously reported similar systems (Table S1 in the ESM). In comparison, 3D-MOF-Re exhibited an inferior TON of 0.55 just like other previously reported 3D-MOF (Table S1 in the ESM). Moreover, no liquid products were detected by \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR (Fig. S18 in the ESM). A greater than 50-fold increase in catalytic performance for 2D-MOF-Re over 3D-MOF-Re verifies the critical role of improved light absorbance capacity in driving photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction.
no liquid products were detected by \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR (Fig. S18 in the ESM). A greater than 50-fold increase in catalytic performance for 2D-MOF-Re over 3D-MOF-Re verifies the critical role of improved light absorbance capacity in driving photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction.
Control experiments were conducted using 2D-MOF-Re as the photocatalyst under the same photocatalytic experiment conditions except for the corresponding variables (Fig. 3(b)). No CO was generated when catalytic reactions were performed in dark or without the photocatalyst suggesting that the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion was driven by light irradiation and proceeded under the action of 2D-MOF-Re. When using 2D-MOF as the photocatalyst, only a trace amount of CO was observed, indicating that \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscyc})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) units in 2D-MOF-Re serve as catalytic active sites for the current photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction. To confirm the origin of the carbon, a \({}^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) isotope experiment over 2D-MOF-Re was conducted, and the peak at \(m/z=29\) in the GC-MS spectrum demonstrates that the generated CO indeed originates from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Fig. S19 in the ESM). This was also validated by the lack of CO detection when Ar was used as the reactant. No CO was found without TEOA/BIH, demonstrating the essential role of the electron sacrificial agent in this reaction. After the 2D-MOF-Re photocatalytic reaction, the supernatant was collected and employed as a catalyst for additional photocatalytic testing. Only a minor quantity of CO was produced, indicating that the high catalytic activity of 2D-MOF-Re does not originate from the free dispersion of Re units in the solution.
Furthermore, the photocatalytic experiment was recycled four times to confirm the reusability of the 2D-MOF-Re (Fig. S20 in the ESM). The PXRD pattern of the recovered sample showed clear diffraction peaks (Fig. S21(a) in the ESM) and \(\sim2\%\) of Re detached from 2D-MOF-Re detected by ICP-OES. The recovered sample also showed characteristic \(\nu(\mathrm{CO})\) bands (Fig. S21(b) in the ESM) and TEM elemental mapping (Fig. S21(c) in the ESM) still showed the uniform element distribution. All of these results suggest that the structure and composition of 2D-MOF-Re have not changed significantly after the photocatalytic experiment.
## 3.3 Mechanisms of \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction
The mechanism of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction was studied by photophysical and electrochemical investigations. The ultraviolet–visible diffuse reflectance spectrum (UV–vis DRS) of 2D-MOF-Re was measured with 2D-MOF as a reference. For 2D-MOF, there is a broad Soret absorption and four Q bands in the region of 200–800 nm (Fig. 4(a)), which inherits well the characteristics of TCPP ligand (Fig. S22 in the ESM). After the coordination of \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpscyc})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) units, the UV–vis DRS spectrum of 2D-MOF-Re exhibits a distinct new absorption peak in 350–470 nm region due to the light absorption resulting from the introduction of Re units (Fig. 4(a) and Fig. S22 in the ESM). In contrast, both 3D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF exhibit nearly identical UV–vis absorption spectra, suggesting that the introduction of
\( \mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpcy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl} \) has minimal effect on the UV–vis absorption characteristics of the material (Fig. 4(a)). This observation highlights that bulky nature of 3D materials can result in poor light absorption of functional units.
Evaluated by the Tauc plots, the band gap energies \( \left(E_{\mathrm{g}}\right) \) of 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re were determined to be 1.80 and 1.87 eV, respectively (Fig. S23 in the ESM). Meanwhile, Mott–Schottky measurements were conducted to clarify the semiconductor character of the samples (Fig. 4(b) and Fig. S24 in the ESM). The positive slope of the obtained \( C^{2} \) values (vs. the applied potentials) verifies the character of n-type semiconductors of 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re [18, 53]. Besides, the flat band positions of 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re are \( -0.87 \) and \( -0.79 \) \( V_{\mathrm{SCE}} \) (i.e., \( -0.63 \) and \( -0.55 \) \( V_{\mathrm{NHE}} \)) determined from the intersection, which are approximately equal to their conduction band (CB) potentials [15]. Based on the equation \( E_{\mathrm{g}}=E_{\mathrm{VB}}-E_{\mathrm{CB}} \), the valence band (VB) potentials of 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re were calculated to be 1.17 and 1.32 \( V_{\mathrm{NHE}} \), respectively. Obviously, they are thermodynamically feasible for the photocatalytic reduction of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) to CO under visible light.
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the samples were measured to characterize the electron transfer and recombination efficiency of photoexcited electron–hole pairs. As presented in Fig. 4(c), compared to \( \mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl} \), the PL
intensities of 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re are remarkably weakened, and that of 2D-MOF-Re is the weakest, suggesting the presence of electron transfer behavior and the lowest recombination efficiency of photogenerated electrons and holes in 2D-MOF-Re. This efficient photoinduced electron–hole separation greatly facilitates the effective photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction of 2D-MOF-Re. Time-resolved PL (TRPL) spectra were further measured to determine the lifetime of the photoexcited electron–hole pairs. After fitting with a biexponential equation (Fig. 4(d)), a relatively longer average lifetime \( \left(T_{\mathrm{avg}}\right) \) was achieved for 2D-MOF-Re (3.83 ns) than that of 3D-MOF-Re (3.51 ns), indicating that the photogenerated charge carriers on the surface of 2D-MOF-Re survived longer (Table S2 in the ESM). These results illustrate that the dimensional reduction structures can reduce the recombination of photoinduced electron–hole pairs and promote their separation and migration in the framework, which is beneficial to more efficient photocatalytic activity.
Based on the above studies and reported literature [54–57], possible mechanisms regarding the photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction of 2D-MOF-Re (Fig. 5) and 3D-MOF-Re (Fig. S25 in the ESM) are proposed. For 2D-MOF-Re, the low dimensionality of 2D-MOF promotes the direct coordination of carboxyl groups in catalytic centers \( \mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpcy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl} \) with the \( Z_{\mathrm{R}_{6}} \) clusters in the MOF. Under visible light irradiation, the exposed
\(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpsc y})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) was excited to generate electron–hole pairs. Then the photoinduced electrons were transferred to Re centers to reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO product. Meanwhile, the photogenerated holes were recombined with the electrons offered by BIH/TEOA to complete the photocatalytic cycle.
## 4 Conclusions
In this work, we have revealed a significant enhancement of photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction performance via dimensional reduction of metal-organic frameworks. Based on the rational design, the \(\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{bpy})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}\) catalytic units were separately anchored within 2D and 3D MOFs formed by porphyrins and \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters affording 2D-MOF-Re and 3D-MOF-Re. Their sufficiently large pores effectively minimize the impact of mass transfer processes on catalytic reactions. Photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction experimental results reveal that the TON of 2D-MOF-Re can reach 27.8 within \(6\,\mathrm{{h}}\) , which is 50 times that of 3D-MOF-Re with an equal amount of Re. This implies although there are catalytic active sites within the bulk-phase MOFs, the functionality of some of them remains inactive due to the inability to absorb light. This work not only sheds light on the potential of dimensional reduction strategy in the field of photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction but also highlights the significance of rational design in harnessing the full potential of nanoscale photocatalysts.
